WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
The first film followed energy through the Bay.

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.480
Do real measurements shrink in the same neat pattern?

3
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.060
Test the model with one measured ecosystem.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.500
The measurements come from Silver Springs, Florida.

5
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.880
Researchers estimated yearly energy entering each feeding level.

6
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.320
The values were rough, but they let us test the pyramid's shape.

7
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.740
Each value combines many lives across a shared area.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.703
Start with energy captured by plants and algae.

9
00:00:36.043 --> 00:00:38.143
Their green bar fills most of the frame.

10
00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:42.943
This is the producer level and the base for the consumer paths.

11
00:00:44.303 --> 00:00:47.063
Everything above depends on this incoming supply.

12
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.113
Now add the consumers in order.

13
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:54.013
Each measured bar is narrower than the one below.

14
00:00:55.293 --> 00:00:58.533
A narrower bar means less energy entered that level.

15
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:02.613
Yet the bars do not shrink by one identical fraction.

16
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:08.043
Calculate each transfer by dividing a level by the one below it.

17
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:12.943
Each result describes only one jump between neighboring levels.

18
00:01:14.163 --> 00:01:16.403
A fixed law would make every result match.

19
00:01:17.663 --> 00:01:19.803
The uneven bars already hint otherwise.

20
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:25.483
Will the three calculated percentages match exactly, or will they differ?

21
00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:28.463
Choose before the results appear.

22
00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:31.563
Use each neighboring pair in the stack.

23
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:43.590
They differ. The three measured steps land in different places around the model

24
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.290
marker. Real transfer varies even within one ecosystem.

25
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.630
No single measured step defines the whole web.

26
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.880
The missing energy did not vanish.

27
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.420
Organisms released much as heat during life processes.

28
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.820
Uneaten material and wastes carried stored energy into a decomposer side branch.

29
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.540
Decomposers receive material from the whole web, so they sit beside the stack.

30
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.920
The producer row needs special care.

31
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.800
It counts all captured energy.

32
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.140
Plants used much themselves, so only part became new plant material that consumers

33
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:31.060
could eat. That smaller block is the producer material available for growth and

34
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.060
feeding.

35
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.860
These bars measure energy flow, not animal counts.

36
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.920
One large organism and many small ones can use similar totals.

37
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.600
They came from one spring and rough estimates.

38
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.620
The pattern can guide a claim without predicting an exact population.

39
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.150
Return to the Bay.

40
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.870
Different feeding steps can pass different fractions.

41
00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.930
The exact width may change, but the upward supply narrows.

42
00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.930
Repeating those losses still leaves far less energy near an osprey than near the

43
00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:07.930
producers.

44
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.880
The measurements refine the classroom model without changing its main pattern.

45
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.980
At each feeding step, less energy is available to the next level.

46
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.300
Use the rule for direction, then use data for size.

47
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.020
Every step passes on less energy, but how much less varies.
